# WALL(1M)

## NAME

wall - write to all users

## SYNOPSIS

| **/etc/wall** [ *file* ]

## DESCRIPTION

*Wall* reads its standard input until an end-of-file, or the named *file* if one is given. It
then sends this message, preceded by `Broadcast Message ...`, to all logged in users.

The sender should be super-user to override any protections the users may have invoked.

**Note:** that override is nothing but the ordinary permission check on opening the terminal,
which mesg(1) has set to 0600 or 0622. *Wall* makes no check of its own - unlike write(1),
which refuses even the super-user on a terminal that has said no. For the same reason *wall* is
not set-user-id: the bit would hand the override to everybody and leave nothing to invoke.

**Note:** the file argument is not in the SYNOPSIS of v7's page and has always been in its
program. Users are served in the slot order of /etc/utmp - which ttyslot(3) makes the line
order of /etc/ttys - one forked child apiece, a second apart. The children are not waited for.

**Note:** the message is held in a 3000-character buffer. v7 read past the end of it without a
word; a longer message is truncated here and says so on the standard error.

## FILES

- /dev/tty?
- /etc/utmp
- /etc/ttys

## SEE ALSO

mesg(1), write(1), who(1), utmp(5)

## DIAGNOSTICS

`cannot open ...` when the open on a user's tty file fails.

**Note:** this page said `Cannot send to ...`; the program has always written `cannot open`.
